<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yucatán Peninsula</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yucatán Peninsula is a low limestone platform in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, separating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Sea. It is divided among the Mexican states of Yucatán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintana-roo">Quintana Roo</a>, together with Belize and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Lacking surface rivers, it depended on cenotes for water, and it was a heartland of Maya civilisation, with cities such as Chichén Itzá, Uxmal, Cobá and Mayapán.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula.jpg" alt="Yucatán Peninsula" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nasa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yucatan_peninsula_250m.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
